WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.685
The story of higher education is changing.

00:00:03.685 --> 00:00:09.784
What students complete, where potential learners live, and what a degree costs are

00:00:09.784 --> 00:00:10.723
all moving.

00:00:10.723 --> 00:00:13.069
Can universities adapt quickly enough?

00:00:14.669 --> 00:00:20.568
Start with the fields in which American universities award bachelor's degrees.

00:00:20.568 --> 00:00:24.321
Compare selected fields in 2014 and 2024.

00:00:24.321 --> 00:00:29.683
Their rankings change as some fields expand and others contract.

00:00:29.683 --> 00:00:34.509
These are two observed snapshots, not an annual series.

00:00:34.509 --> 00:00:38.263
Each bar represents degrees awarded, not applications.

00:00:39.863 --> 00:00:43.504
A ranking only shows part of the story.

00:00:43.504 --> 00:00:49.420
Compare each selected field's share of all bachelor's awards in 2014 and 2024.

00:00:49.420 --> 00:00:55.337
Completions tell us where students finished their studies, with a delay after their

00:00:55.337 --> 00:00:56.247
original choices.

00:00:57.647 --> 00:01:02.081
Universities have to respond to changing fields of study.

00:01:02.081 --> 00:01:08.486
That affects program design, teaching capacity, career pathways, and decisions about which opportunities

00:01:08.486 --> 00:01:09.471
to develop.

00:01:10.971 --> 00:01:12.757
Now change the map.

00:01:12.757 --> 00:01:18.561
The number of high school graduates differs across states, and those differences will

00:01:18.561 --> 00:01:19.007
intensify.

00:01:19.007 --> 00:01:23.025
Here we move from historical data to published projections.

00:01:24.625 --> 00:01:30.656
The Western Interstate Commission for Higher Education projects a national peak in high

00:01:30.656 --> 00:01:32.512
school graduates in 2025.

00:01:32.512 --> 00:01:38.079
By 2041, the total is projected to be about thirteen percent lower.

00:01:39.679 --> 00:01:43.398
But the national average hides regional differences.

00:01:43.398 --> 00:01:50.305
Most states are projected to have fewer graduates in 2041 than in 2023.

00:01:50.305 --> 00:01:53.493
Institutional strategy must reflect local conditions.

00:01:54.993 --> 00:01:59.387
Three universities followed different enrollment paths across ten years.

00:01:59.387 --> 00:02:05.734
We will follow Harvard, UNC Charlotte and Salem State, year by year, from

00:02:05.734 --> 00:02:07.199
2016 through 2025.

00:02:07.199 --> 00:02:13.546
Each begins at an index of one hundred, so we can compare changes

00:02:13.546 --> 00:02:14.522
over time.

00:02:16.122 --> 00:02:20.397
Harvard's university-wide degree-student headcount changed unevenly.

00:02:20.397 --> 00:02:29.660
It grew through 2019, declined in 2020, rebounded sharply in 2021, and eased

00:02:29.660 --> 00:02:31.086
after 2022.

00:02:31.086 --> 00:02:36.786
By 2025, enrollment remained above its 2016 level.

00:02:38.286 --> 00:02:42.884
UNC Charlotte expanded over the decade, with interruptions.

00:02:42.884 --> 00:02:49.781
Enrollment rose to 2019, slipped in 2020, and fell again in 2022.

00:02:49.781 --> 00:02:57.252
Then it grew in each of the next three years, reaching 32,207 in

00:02:57.252 --> 00:02:57.827
2025.

00:02:57.827 --> 00:03:01.275
That is a distinct public-university trajectory.

00:03:02.775 --> 00:03:06.080
Salem State experienced a prolonged decline.

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:13.242
Its annual enrollment moved from 9,001 in 2016 to 6,009 in 2025, with

00:03:13.242 --> 00:03:15.996
decreases in every intervening year.

00:03:15.996 --> 00:03:22.056
The university has responded with access, affordability, recruitment and retention initiatives.

00:03:22.056 --> 00:03:27.014
Those actions should be evaluated on their own outcomes.

00:03:28.714 --> 00:03:33.198
Now watch all ten years for all three universities together.

00:03:33.198 --> 00:03:36.786
Their enrollment trends diverge across the same period.

00:03:36.786 --> 00:03:41.718
The lines show what happened, but they do not establish why.

00:03:41.718 --> 00:03:47.548
To understand institutional effectiveness, we must also ask what happens after students arrive.

00:03:49.048 --> 00:03:52.440
The three institutions serve different student markets.

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:57.285
Now move from institutional headcounts to the people behind them.

00:03:57.285 --> 00:04:00.677
Follow a national cohort after students start.

00:04:02.177 --> 00:04:05.552
Imagine one hundred students entering higher education.

00:04:05.552 --> 00:04:10.373
Each dot represents one person in a national starting cohort.

00:04:10.373 --> 00:04:13.266
Move the clock forward six years.

00:04:14.966 --> 00:04:21.615
Of every hundred starters in the fall 2019 cohort, roughly sixty-one completed a

00:04:21.615 --> 00:04:23.661
credential within six years.

00:04:23.661 --> 00:04:25.707
Nine were still enrolled.

00:04:25.707 --> 00:04:28.776
About thirty were no longer enrolled.

00:04:28.776 --> 00:04:31.845
Every pathway has a different consequence.

00:04:34.145 --> 00:04:37.614
Real students encounter challenges at different times.

00:04:37.614 --> 00:04:44.058
An unexpected bill, a course they cannot take, or support that arrives too

00:04:44.058 --> 00:04:46.040
late can disrupt progress.

00:04:46.040 --> 00:04:49.014
These examples illustrate possible friction points.

00:04:50.414 --> 00:04:53.593
These challenges cross institutional boundaries.

00:04:53.593 --> 00:05:01.859
Admissions, finance, advising, registration, teaching, wellbeing, and careers all shape the learner experience.

00:05:01.859 --> 00:05:08.218
Their connections deserve the same attention as their individual performance.

00:05:09.718 --> 00:05:13.149
Watch a student move through disconnected offices.

00:05:13.149 --> 00:05:18.050
Each handoff creates waiting time, repeated explanations, or missed information.

00:05:18.050 --> 00:05:24.422
When information stays isolated, the next team may recognize the problem too late.

00:05:25.822 --> 00:05:27.802
Now connect those offices.

00:05:27.802 --> 00:05:31.267
An early signal reaches the right owner.

00:05:31.267 --> 00:05:35.721
The team coordinates an academic, financial, or wellbeing response.

00:05:35.721 --> 00:05:40.176
Progress is reviewed, and the plan changes when needed.

00:05:41.776 --> 00:05:47.231
A strong learning system rests on five foundations.

00:05:47.231 --> 00:05:49.277
High quality teaching.

00:05:49.277 --> 00:05:50.641
Study skills.

00:05:50.641 --> 00:05:52.686
Spaces to learn.

00:05:52.686 --> 00:05:54.050
Personal development.

00:05:54.050 --> 00:05:55.414
Professional development.

00:05:55.414 --> 00:05:59.505
Each foundation supports progress toward completion.

00:06:01.105 --> 00:06:07.178
Run the student journey again, this time with clearer pathways and coordinated support.

00:06:07.178 --> 00:06:09.981
Some illustrated risks are addressed earlier.

00:06:09.981 --> 00:06:16.054
This is a design scenario, not a measured prediction of improved graduation rates.

00:06:17.654 --> 00:06:21.045
Now follow the financial side of that journey.

00:06:21.045 --> 00:06:26.555
Every additional term can bring tuition, living expenses, and time away from full

00:06:26.555 --> 00:06:27.402
time work.

00:06:27.402 --> 00:06:30.793
The cost accumulates before a credential is earned.

00:06:32.293 --> 00:06:38.526
Three students can enter the same program and leave with very different outcomes.

00:06:38.526 --> 00:06:40.444
One completes on time.

00:06:40.444 --> 00:06:41.883
Another takes longer.

00:06:41.883 --> 00:06:44.760
A third leaves without a credential.

00:06:44.760 --> 00:06:47.157
Their costs and opportunities diverge.

00:06:48.657 --> 00:06:53.946
National labor market data show a clear difference across educational attainment groups.

00:06:53.946 --> 00:06:59.677
In 2025, median weekly earnings were higher for workers with bachelor's degrees than

00:06:59.677 --> 00:07:03.203
for workers with some college and no degree.

00:07:03.203 --> 00:07:07.611
These are group medians, not a forecast for any individual.

00:07:09.411 --> 00:07:13.416
The economic question becomes more difficult when completion is uncertain.

00:07:13.416 --> 00:07:18.622
How much should someone invest in a degree, what support can they expect,

00:07:18.622 --> 00:07:21.425
and what happens if they cannot finish?

00:07:23.225 --> 00:07:27.095
The answer requires a system that learns.

00:07:27.095 --> 00:07:29.306
Demand informs academic programs.

00:07:29.306 --> 00:07:31.517
Student signals inform support.

00:07:31.517 --> 00:07:35.939
Completion and career outcomes inform the next decisions.

00:07:35.939 --> 00:07:39.809
Every cycle creates an opportunity to improve.

00:07:41.509 --> 00:07:48.206
Twenty-first-century enrollment leadership designs the full learner relationship, from first interest through graduation,

00:07:48.206 --> 00:07:50.782
career outcomes, and alumni connection.

00:07:50.782 --> 00:07:55.418
The university works best when its systems work together.
